hello my name is amelia chong and i'm a  senior at holt high school in winterfell  missouri  i want to share with you my project  analyzing genes relating to reactive  oxygen species  and stylophora pistolata corals under  short-term thermal stress  climate change is causing corals to die  at a devastating rate  this process is the result of the  disruption of the symbiotic relationship  between corals and algae resulting in  coral bleaching  it is commonly believed that the  overproduction of reactive oxygen  species by the coral symbionts  is responsible for bleaching but titles  of this topic are still under  investigation  a better understanding of the  progression of ros production over time  in relation to other bleaching factors  could benefit future studies of coral  bleaching short-term thermal stress is  reversible for some coral speech  species which might indicate that the  widespread bleaching of light could be  reversible  but a more detailed study of this  ecological microsystem is needed to  understand what measures could be done  to stop  slow down or reverse this process  to undertake this project i reviewed  literature on research in this field  and found that the latest projects  utilized molecular signatures from  microarray and next generation  sequencing data to study this phenomenon  as a result i see that the current  understanding of ros is that it is  the trigger of bleaching and  specifically in this study is a result  of increased  temperature from 24 degrees celsius to  34 degrees celsius  over a 10-day period at the molecular  level  this steep increase in temperature leads  to a cascade of bleaching related  processes  in dinoflagellates in the coral itself  to understand this phenomenon  i wanted to collect raw sequencing data  that would allow me to analyze gene  expression patterns of genes related to  reactive oxygen species  and stylophoropistillatocoral under  short-term thermal stress  before i tell you about my analysis  there are several terms that you need to  know  personally i've been throwing around the  phrase reactive oxygen species or ros  but what are they ros are highly  reactive molecules that can cause death  uh cell damage or death if built up in  the cell ros are the cause of the next  term  oxidative stress which is the term for  when ros  build up in a system which in this case  is the coral and the coral is then  unable to  detoxify itself and repair the damage  caused by the ros  oxidoreductase is an enzyme that helps  to reduce oxygen into a less harmful  compound  and mitigates the overproduction of ros  and then after that we have log two fold  change which is which is the data that  i'm working with  this is a quantity that compares gene  expression values and can reveal  if a gene is up or down regulated  i went to the national center for  biotechnology information's gene  expression omnibus  and found 179 data sets related to the  to coral heat stress out of the 179 data  sets  18 described gene expression using  microarray or high throughput sequencing  gene expression studies  i collected eight data sets that are  relevant to my problem statement and  performed exploratory analysis  in the exploratory analysis i found that  samples could be separated by  temperature levels so i define my  hypothesis that there are genes that  will be up or down regulated  in low and high temperature environments  for a broad range of different corals  after exploring the data sets i selected  the data set gse477  taken from the publication gene  expression profile of heat stress  in the red sea coral styrofoam pistolata  by moore landau at all  in that study the stylophora pistol out  of coral are separated into four  different groups exposed to four  different temperatures  24 degrees celsius which is the control  and then the treatment groups 28 degrees  celsius  32 degrees celsius and 40 degrees  celsius which i will be referring to  as 28 32 and 34 respectively  and from that data set i search for  genes that are differentially expressed  with a threshold of 0.05 p  value and above 1.2 log-2 fold change  then i annotated for ontology function  with uniprot and filtered for genes  related to  bleaching for each sample group  comparison  using the gene ontology information i  search for the key terms oxidative  repair  and oxytocidal reductase the results  show a large increase in the amount of  differentially expressed genes from the  low to medium treatment temperatures  a small decrease in the amount of  differentially expressed genes from the  medium to high treatment temperatures  giving an indicator for what significant  change starts to happen in coral  bleaching  i conducted t-tests with a threshold  p-value of 0.05 to compare the results  of each treatment for each filter term  for the up for both upregulated and the  downregulated data sets  the results supported the observation of  a stark contrast between the number of  genes differentially expressed  from the 28 treatment groups compared to  the 34 32 and 34 treatment groups for  all the filter terms of the upregulated  genes  when comparing the 32 and 34 treatments  the only filter term that yielded a  p-value below 0.05  was oxidative when looking at the  down-regulated set of genes  the oxidative term had a significant  difference between  the 28 and 32 agreement treatment groups  as well as the 32 and 34 treatment  groups but not for the 28  versus the 34 treatment groups possibly  indicating that the oxidative related  responses for treatment groups  28 and 34 were similar to each other and  different from treatment 32.  none of the other comparisons of  downregulated genes displayed a  significant p-value  even though next-generation sequencing  provides a greater dynamic range for  studying gene expression  and allows for more accurate  measurements of gene expression changes  microarray data can be analyzed and used  more easily due to the lack of annotated  coral genomes and gene function  in literature additional factors like  genetic background of corals  prior prior exposure to short-term  thermal stress  additional microbes in the microbiome  and their influence on the algae and  corals are mentioned  the study did not consider these  additional factors additionally this  study  this particular study is only three to  five samples per condition so it's not  as comprehensive as i would like it to  be  in the article which produced this data  set the authors wanted to understand  gene pathways associated with heat  stress when i started my analysis i  already had  a specific question about the  relationship between the coral and algae  so i focus on a different aspect of the  overall response to the heat stress  both this study and the original study  found that reactions to heat just began  during the 32 treatment but the original  study found that the reactions intensify  significantly  with the 34 treatment while this study  showed a slight decrease in reactions  this variation in multiple and results  could be caused by multiple different  factors  firstly the original study took into  account all genes while this study only  focused on a specific group of genes  secondly any genes that could not be  annotated with uniprot were omitted from  the study  and lastly analysis for the study was  performed using excel rather than using  a more powerful analysis tool such as r  in the future i would like to use a good  reference gmo genome to annotate next  generation sequencing data so i can do  this analysis in  more in depth and with less possibility  for mistakes by not using excel  additionally i would like to incorporate  more variation in species  and look at genetic data and microbiome  data to show relationship between  multiple factors for biological  validation of my analysis  lastly i would like to look into  mechanics mechanistic studies  that look at coral oxidal reductase in  response to ros levels and algae
 

